# Working in `src/language`

Layout:
- `src/language/minab.langium` — the grammar. Hand-written, tracked in git. Mirrors the reference grammar in spec §11 exactly — the two must never drift.
- `src/language/minab-module.ts` — Langium DI module wiring, built on `langium/lsp`'s services (`LangiumServices`, since Phase 7) rather than the core-only ones. `MinabServices` includes an `lsp` service group (`HoverProvider`/`DefinitionProvider`, see `src/language/lsp/`) in addition to the custom services below.
- `src/language/main.ts` — the language server entry point (Phase 7): `createConnection` + `startLanguageServer`, with one `MinabSchema`/`MinabRuleContext` for the whole process, discovered from `minab.config.json` the same way the CLI does (`src/cli/config.ts`'s `discoverConfig`).
- `src/language/lsp/` — `MinabHoverProvider`/`MinabDefinitionProvider` (Phase 7): hand-written, not the Langium defaults, since Minab has no Langium cross-references for the default reference-based providers to key off. Both resolve `#alias` via `MinabScopeResolver.resolveNamedScope` — the same resolution the `Validator`'s `NamedScope` check already uses.
- `vscode-extension/` — a minimal VS Code extension (Phase 7), a separate non-workspace npm package with its own `node_modules`/build. `src/extension.ts` starts a `LanguageClient` pointed at the root package's compiled `out/src/language/main.js` by relative path. Syntax highlighting comes from a TextMate grammar `langium generate` now also emits (`langium-config.json`'s `textMate.out`), not hand-written.
- `src/language/generated/` — output of `langium generate`. Gitignored. Never hand-edit; regenerate instead.
- `src/language/schema.ts` — the host-supplied schema contract (`MinabSchema`/`SchemaProvider`: tables, columns, built-in function signatures). Minab has no in-file table declarations — this is the only source of truth for what `#alias`/bare table names mean, and it's genuinely per-embedding data, passed into `createMinabServices(context, schema)` rather than hardcoded.
- `src/language/minab-scope-resolver.ts` — `MinabScopeResolver`, resolution for the scope-stack sigils (`.`/`^`/`#alias`/`KEY`, spec §2.2) plus bare `NameRef` lookups. Not a Langium `ScopeProvider`/Linker hookup — see the file's own header comment for why, and `docs/roadmap.md` Phase 2 for the full story. Since Phase 4, also resolves a `.field`-rooted receiver's own target table (`.orders[...]`'s `.orders`, previously punted), via `tableOfExpression`/`followRelationColumn`.
- `src/language/minab-validator.ts` — `MinabValidator`, the Langium `Validator` (Phase 3: `$`/`KEY`/`#alias` placement; Phase 4: the full type-checking diagnostic set, built on `minab-type-checker.ts`). See the file's own header comment for the exact check list.
- `src/language/minab-types.ts` — `MinabType` (Phase 4): the structured type representation (`scalar`/`tuple`/`record`/`collection`/`null`) used by both `schema.ts`'s column/function types and `MinabTypeChecker`'s inference output, plus assignability/coercion helpers (`isAssignableTo`, `baseTypesEqual`, `widenNumeric`, ...).
- `src/language/minab-builtins.ts` — the 8 built-in aggregate/predicate function signatures (`COUNT`/`SUM`/`AVG`/`MIN`/`MAX`/`EXISTS`/`ALL`/`ANY`, spec §5.3.1) — reserved names, called bare, never `&`-prefixed.
- `src/language/minab-type-checker.ts` — `MinabTypeChecker` (Phase 4): `inferType(node: Expression)` over the full expression grammar, following `MinabScopeResolver`'s `ScopeResolution`/`{ok,reason}` pattern. See the file's own header comment for the traversal/coercion/null rules it implements.
- `src/language/minab-sql-compiler.ts` — `MinabSqlCompiler` (Phase 5): compiles the *relational* layer to parameterized SQL — a `Query` pipeline, ad-hoc `#Table` scopes, relation traversal, built-in aggregates over either. Its `{ok:false, reason}` for `&fn`/`if`/`switch`/loops is deliberate and load-bearing, not a gap: it's how the interpreter knows to take a node itself. See `docs/adr/0001-execution-strategy.md`.
- `src/language/minab-interpreter.ts` — `MinabInterpreter` (Phase 5): evaluates everything else against the record the host holds, pushing the smallest table-touching subexpression down to the compiler. Async throughout (every `fn` is implicitly async, spec §8). `EvalContext.onStatement(query, origin)` (2026-09-19) tells a host which AST node each statement was compiled from — the playground's execution map; it must stay called immediately before `executor.execute`.
- `src/language/minab-executor.ts` — `QueryExecutor`/`SqlQuery`, the host connection contract. Same "host supplies what Minab source can't say" shape as `schema.ts`, but passed per call to `interpreter.evaluate` rather than to `createMinabServices`, since a connection is per-invocation, not per-embedding.
- `test/parsing.test.ts` — vitest smoke suite, using `parseHelper` from `langium/test`. Structured as one `describe` block per section of `docs/showcase.md` (one `describe` per showcase section) — every construct in the showcase should have a passing parse example here, plus a dedicated block confirming that examples the showcase flags as *semantic* errors still parse cleanly.
- `test/scoping.test.ts` — resolution tests for `MinabScopeResolver` (12 tests as of 2026-09-14), using a small fixture `MinabSchema` rather than a real host schema. Separate from the parsing smoke suite since it asserts on resolution results, not just clean parsing.
- `test/validation.test.ts` — `MinabValidator`'s Phase 3 checks (`$`/`KEY`/`#alias` placement), using `validationHelper` from `langium/test` and a fixture `MinabSchema`.
- `test/typechecking.test.ts` — the Phase 4 type-checking diagnostic set, same `validationHelper` pattern, its own broader fixture schema (multiple tables, `ref`/`collection` columns, `JSON`).
- `test/sql-compilation.test.ts` — Phase 5 codegen, generated SQL diffed against hand-written expected SQL. When you change what the compiler emits, the diff is supposed to fail: update the expected string deliberately rather than loosening the assertion.
- `test/evaluation.test.ts` — Phase 5 end to end, using a recording `QueryExecutor` fixture. Asserts the *strategy* (a correlated `#Table` check leaves as one parameterized `EXISTS`; a rule the interpreter can settle alone issues no query at all), not just the returned value.
- `src/host/` — the browser-safe half of the CLI's host (2026-09-19, for the web playground): `config.ts` (`parseConfig` — the `minab.config.json` shape, with no file system; a string path is rejected unless the caller passes `readJson`), `fixture-executor.ts` (`FixtureExecutor`, `traced`, `DataSourceError`), `format.ts` (`formatSql`/`formatValue`). No `node:*` imports allowed here — the playground bundles it for the browser. `src/cli/` re-exports these, so existing imports keep working.
- `playground/` — the web playground (2026-09-19): a standalone Vite + React package, like `vscode-extension/` (own `package.json`/`node_modules`, no workspace). It compiles `src/language/` and `src/host/` straight from source (Vite `resolve.dedupe` keeps one Langium copy), runs the engine in a Web Worker with PGlite as the database, and has its own vitest suite that runs every gallery example and tour lesson. `playground/design/` holds the design brief, flows, screen specs, the design contract and the Claude Design prompts. See `playground/README.md`.
- `src/cli/` — the `minab` command (Phase 6): `main.ts` (`check`/`compile`/`run`, arg parsing, result rendering), `bin.ts` (the shebang wrapper; `package.json`'s `bin` points at the built `out/src/cli/bin.js`), `config.ts` (`minab.config.json` — the host stand-in: schema, rule context, record, canned data, connection string; the file-system half — parsing lives in `src/host/config.ts`), `executors.ts` (the PostgreSQL `QueryExecutor`; the fixture one lives in `src/host/`), `diagnostics.ts` (`file:line:col` + caret rendering). It adds no language behavior — everything is a front end over the services above. `runCli(argv, io)` takes its output sinks as parameters so tests drive whole commands in-process.
- `examples/<name>/` — one `.minab` program and its own `minab.config.json` per directory (config is found by walking up from a program's own directory, not from the working directory). Adding one means adding it to the manifest in `test/examples.test.ts`, or the drift guard fails. Eight run against their fixture; three (`overdue-loop`, `order-dml`, `reconcile-overdue-accounts`) are check-only until loops/writes execute.
- `test/examples.test.ts` — Phase 8: every example driven through `runCli` (check clean, `run --json` equals the documented answer, `--trace` statement count, check-only examples must be *refused* by `run`), plus a check that the README's output blocks match real CLI output.
- `test/cli.test.ts` — the CLI, asserted on what a user sees: exit codes (0 fine / 1 bad-or-failed program / 2 bad invocation), messages, the caret, the table.
- `test/postgres.test.ts` — the compiled SQL actually **executed**, against a real PostgreSQL. Opt-in: set `MINAB_TEST_DATABASE_URL` to a throwaway database (it drops and recreates its tables), otherwise the whole file skips. Everything else only ever diffs generated SQL against expected text, so this is the only place a "valid SQL?" question gets a real answer.
- `test/lsp.test.ts` — `MinabHoverProvider`/`MinabDefinitionProvider` (Phase 7), called directly against a `parseHelper`-built document — no real JSON-RPC connection needed, same pattern as the scoping/validation test files.

Workflow for any grammar change:
1. Edit `src/language/minab.langium`.
2. `npm run langium:generate` — regenerates the AST, grammar, and module in `src/language/generated`. Treat any ambiguity/conflict warning as worth understanding, not just "did it exit 0" — the one existing warning (`Postfix`'s `TupleAccess` vs. `FilterAccess` over a bare `[NUMBER]`) is a known, accepted one (spec §12 item 4), not something to silently add to.
3. `npm run build` — typechecks against the regenerated AST types.
4. Add or update a case in `test/parsing.test.ts` covering the new syntax against `docs/showcase.md`'s own example for it, then `npm test`. **Clean generation and a clean build are not proof the grammar is right** — actually run the parse test. The 2026-09-13 v2 integration passed `langium generate`/`tsc -b` without a single error, and the test suite still caught three real defects (see `docs/roadmap.md` Phase 1): a clause-order regression, a `CurrentRecord` node missing a `vivify` flag that a headline spec example depended on, and an operator misused against an operand its own grammar rule doesn't accept. When something doesn't parse, don't assume it's a Chevrotain lookahead bug before checking the simpler explanation — re-read the exact grammar rule for the operator/construct involved; two of those three turned out to be plain example mistakes once checked against the grammar's own definition, not parser defects.
5. Only after 2-4 are green, and only with the approval described in the spec-governance rule, update `docs/query-language-spec.md` (§11's grammar block, plus whatever prose sections the change affects) and `docs/showcase.md` (a runnable example for the new construct, in its own numbered section, cross-referencing back to the spec section). Section numbers and cross-references need to stay consistent in both files; that's part of the change, not a follow-up.

Parser mode: `createMinabServices` takes an optional `{ mode: 'production' }`, which only the CLI passes. In Langium's `development` mode (what `langium generate` writes into `generated/module.ts`) Chevrotain re-validates the whole grammar on every parser construction — that is where lookahead/ambiguity warnings come from, and it costs ~2.8s per services instance against this grammar; `production` skips it and takes ~80ms. Leave the test suite and any grammar work in `development`: that check is the safety net step 4 relies on.

Debugging a parse failure: when `expectNoErrors`-style assertions fail with a generic "Expecting end of file but found `X`" message, that only tells you *where* the parser gave up, not *why* — a failure deep inside a nested function/loop/if can surface as an error at the very first token of the whole document, because the outer rule's parse attempt aborts entirely and error recovery reports its own entry point. Bisect by extracting the smallest snippet that still fails (a temporary `describe('debug', ...)` block with a few `console.log`-ing test cases works well, deleted once done) before concluding it's a genuine grammar defect rather than a simple misuse of some other construct.

Current implementation status (update this line as work lands, don't let it go stale): full v2 grammar (types, functions, control flow, loops, DML) plus core Langium services, verified against `docs/showcase.md`'s 49-case suite as of 2026-09-13. Sigil resolution (`src/language/minab-scope-resolver.ts`, `MinabScopeResolver`, since 2026-09-14) resolves `.`/`^`/`#alias`/`KEY` (spec §2) against the §2.2 scope stack — still *not* a Langium `ScopeProvider` wired into the Linker, since nothing in the grammar uses cross-reference (`[Type:ID]`) syntax; table/column/function metadata comes from a host-supplied `MinabSchema`/`SchemaProvider` (`src/language/schema.ts`) passed to `createMinabServices`, not in-file declarations. As of 2026-09-16 (Phase 3), a `Validator` (`src/language/minab-validator.ts`) exists and catches `$`/`KEY`/`#alias` placement errors. As of 2026-09-17 (Phase 4), a real type system exists: `src/language/minab-types.ts`'s structured `MinabType` replaced `schema.ts`'s free-form column-type string; `src/language/minab-type-checker.ts`'s `MinabTypeChecker` infers types over the full expression grammar (multi-hop `ref` traversal, `collection` broadcast, built-in/user function resolution); and `MinabValidator` now also enforces no-implicit-coercion, the §3.4 collection-vs-scalar boundary, and the §7.7 null-operand rules — backed by `test/typechecking.test.ts`. As of 2026-09-17 (Phase 5), Minab also *runs*: `docs/adr/0001-execution-strategy.md` chose a hybrid — `minab-sql-compiler.ts` compiles the relational layer to parameterized SQL, `minab-interpreter.ts` evaluates the rest against a host-supplied record and pushes the smallest table-touching subexpression down to the compiler. `schema.ts` grew `primaryKey`/`foreignKey` for this (executing a traversal needs to know *how* two tables link, not just what the target is). Still not executed, each failing with an explicit reason rather than a wrong answer: loops (§9.4), DML execution (§10), `.$index` (§3.5) — see `docs/roadmap.md` Phase 5 and `docs/status.md`'s "Next job". As of 2026-09-17 (Phase 6), there is a CLI (`src/cli/`, `minab check`/`compile`/`run`) over all of the above, and the generated SQL has been executed against a real PostgreSQL for the first time (`test/postgres.test.ts`, opt-in). As of 2026-09-18 (Phase 7), Minab has real IDE tooling: `minab-module.ts` is built on `langium/lsp`, `src/language/main.ts` is a language server, `src/language/lsp/` adds hand-written hover/go-to-definition for `#alias`, and `vscode-extension/` is a minimal VS Code extension with a generated TextMate grammar for syntax highlighting. The duplicate-diagnostics issue `MinabValidator` had carried since Phase 6 is fixed at the validator (`MinabTypeChecker.inferType`'s `TypeResult` now carries an `origin` node, reported exactly once); the CLI's old post-processing workaround (`dedupe` in `src/cli/main.ts`) is gone. As of 2026-09-19 (Phase 8), the spec is marked Stable and `examples/<name>/` holds eleven tested programs (see above); one known bug is open — a top-level rule filtering a collection (`COUNT(.orders[...]) < 5`) fails type-checking without a surrounding `FROM`, tracked in `docs/status.md`'s "Next job".

Don't add a `ScopeProvider`/`Validator`/other custom service by guessing Langium's current API shape — check the installed `langium` version's types first (`node_modules/langium/lib/**/*.d.ts` or the generated `module.ts` for the exact imports it already uses), since Langium's service surface has moved between major versions (this project is on `langium` ~4.4, core services only).
